.TH FFMPEG-TUI 1 "2026" "ffmpeg-tui" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ffmpeg-tui \- keyboard-driven terminal interface for FFmpeg
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ffmpeg-tui
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ffmpeg-tui
is a terminal user interface for the official
.BR ffmpeg (1)
and
.BR ffprobe (1)
command line tools. It browses media, inspects streams, builds conversions with
a guided wizard, manages a job queue, and runs FFmpeg directly without a shell.
.PP
Every screen shows the exact argv that will be executed before anything runs.
FFmpeg is always started with an explicit argument vector; filenames are never
re-parsed by a shell, brace expansion, or command substitution.
.PP
Only one job runs at a time. Jobs can be paused, resumed, canceled, retried and
reordered. Pausing uses POSIX job-control signals and is therefore unavailable
on Windows.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-dir \fIPATH\fR
Start in \fIPATH\fR instead of the current directory.
.TP
.B \-ffmpeg \fIPATH\fR
Use a specific \fBffmpeg\fR binary.
.TP
.B \-ffprobe \fIPATH\fR
Use a specific \fBffprobe\fR binary.
.TP
.B \-config \fIPATH\fR
Read and write configuration at \fIPATH\fR instead of the platform default.
.TP
.B \-no-color
Disable colour output.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH SCREENS
.TP
.B Dashboard
Jobs, recent projects, favorites, presets, queue and history.
.TP
.B Media browser
Files and folders with \fBffprobe\fR metadata, streams, chapters, attachments
and a terminal-native ASCII frame or waveform preview.
.TP
.B Conversion wizard
Input, output, container, video codec, quality, encoder speed, audio codec,
audio bitrate, subtitles, metadata, filters and a confirmation screen.
.TP
.B Queue
Live frame, FPS, bitrate, time and ETA for the running job, plus pause, resume,
cancel, retry, reorder, save and load.
.TP
.B Filters
Interactive filtergraph builder covering geometry, colour, overlays, timing,
denoise, sharpening, normalisation and more.
.TP
.B Presets
Built-in and user presets for lossless, web, device, archival, animation and
audio-only output.
.TP
.B Batch
Encode a folder recursively with output templates and skip-existing support.
.TP
.B Tools
One-click audio, video and subtitle recipes, each opening the wizard with a
previewed command.
.TP
.B Metadata
Edit container tags and write them with stream copy.
.TP
.B Streams
Inspect every stream, disposition, chapter and attachment.
.TP
.B Compare
Compare input and output resolution, codec, bitrate, size and compression.
.TP
.B Manual
Offline searchable index of filters, encoders, muxers, demuxers, protocols,
options and tools. Live help can be fetched from the installed FFmpeg.
.TP
.B History
Every executed command with its exit status; replay, delete or export as a
shell script.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+N
New conversion.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q, Ctrl+C
Quit. Active jobs ask for confirmation.
.TP
.B /
Global search across files, manual pages, presets, history and actions.
.TP
.B :
Command palette. For example
.B :man crop
opens the bundled documentation,
.B :man libx264
and
.B :man mp4
open the matching pages.
.TP
.B ?
Help overlay.
.TP
.B Space
Pause or resume the selected job.
.TP
.B Delete
Cancel or remove the selected job.
.TP
.B Tab
Next field, next category, or path completion.
.TP
.B g, G, PgUp, PgDn
Jump to the start or end of a list, or move a page at a time. Lists scroll to
keep the selection visible and show the visible range in the panel title.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ffmpeg-tui/config.json
Configuration on Linux and BSDs (usually
.IR ~/.config/ffmpeg-tui/config.json ).
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/ffmpeg-tui/config.json
Configuration on macOS.
.TP
.I %AppData%\effmpeg-tui\econfig.json
Configuration on Windows.
.TP
.I history.jsonl
Append-only command history, stored beside the configuration file.
.TP
.I queue.json
Saved queue, written with
.B s
on the queue screen.
.TP
.I command-history.sh
Exported shell script of recorded commands.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FFMPEG
Not used; pass
.B \-ffmpeg
or set
.B ffmpeg_path
in the configuration file.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colour when the value is non-empty, in addition to
.BR \-no-color .
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the browser in a folder:
.PP
.nf
    ffmpeg-tui -dir ~/Videos
.fi
.PP
Use a specific FFmpeg build and an isolated configuration:
.PP
.nf
    ffmpeg-tui -ffmpeg /opt/ffmpeg/bin/ffmpeg -config /tmp/tui.json
.fi
.PP
Open the loudness filter documentation immediately:
.PP
.nf
    :man loudnorm
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown.
.TP
.B 1
Startup failure, for example an unreadable configuration directory.
.SH SAFETY
FFmpeg is never invoked through a shell. Paths are validated before a job is
queued: the input must exist, must not be a directory or empty, the output
directory must exist, and input and output must differ. Values placed inside a
filtergraph are escaped for FFmpeg's filtergraph grammar.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1),
.BR ffplay (1)
